// CARD a4-01 · SEE: LEGO's real 2004 highlights table in the dark; a spotlight wanders down its rows; on "twist" it snaps onto the row nobody quotes, profit before special items, and the camera closes in · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@spotlight_row;doc:lego_doc_annualreport_2004_financials;row:.2,.152,.76,.18;go:twist;lab:THE LINE NOBODY QUOTES;sub:LEGO Annual Report 2004 · financial highlights;in:right"
But here's the twist the usual story leaves **out**.

// CARD a4-02 · SEE: a boy building with studded bricks on a living-room floor, dim; on "toy" yellow studs pop onto a green baseplate one per million, 103 of them, as the counter climbs and locks · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@stud_counter;bg:lego_clip_boy_studded_bricks_floor;n:103;go:toy;span:1.4;pre:DKK;lab:MILLION · TOY BUSINESS PROFIT · 2004;sub:before write-downs · LEGO Annual Report 2004;out:right"
In that worst year, the toy business itself, before write-downs, made a **profit**.
One hundred and three million **kroner**.

// CARD a4-03 · SEE: the red brick number 1,931 from the last act hangs at the top; on "cleaning", "write-downs", "restructuring" a share of its bricks pours down into three labelled piles (723, 502, 458); THE REST · 248 stays in the number · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@loss_breakdown_bricks;bg:lego_doc_annualreport_2004_income_statement;num:1,931;total:1931;parts:723^WRITE-DOWNS^cleaning~502^RESTRUCTURING^write-downs~458^CLOSED ACTIVITIES^restructuring;rest:WHAT'S LEFT IN THE LOSS · 248;in:right"
The giant loss came mostly from cleaning up: write-downs, restructuring, and closing what didn't **work**.

// CARD a4-04 · SEE: a plain product box under a checkout scanner, a red laser sweeping it again and again; the readout hunts for a number; on "way" it prints NO DATA in red · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@laser_scan;box:lego_cut_box;go:way;lab:COST PER SET;read:NO DATA;out:right"
The bricks weren't the problem. LEGO had no way to cost a single **set**.

// CARD a4-05 · SEE: a real LEGO kit laid out unbuilt, box top, instruction book and sealed bags, the camera pushing in over the parts · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;under:lego_arch_fallingwater_kit_unbuilt;ua:0;move:in;z:.25;in:right"
Then designers finally went through the sets, one by **one**.

// CARD a4-06 · SEE: the box under the lamp; on "motor" an x-ray band sweeps it and shows gears inside and fibre-optic strands glowing; on "parts" two bars rise: WHAT THE PARTS COST towers over WHAT THE BOX SOLD FOR · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@xray_box;box:lego_cut_box;inner:lego_technic_gears;go:motor;go2:parts;src:LEGO designer Mark Stafford · 2014 interview;out:right"
The worst were sets with a tiny motor or fibre-optic lights: their parts cost more than the whole box sold **for**.

// CARD a4-07 · SEE: the real cover of Brick by Brick on a dark desk; on "maths" the cover swings open and the page reads 1993 → 2002 in pencil, a red ribbon dropping across · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@book_open;doc:lego_doc_book_brickbybrick_crop;go:maths;line1:1993 → 2002;line2:ten years;src:David C. Robertson with Bill Breen · Brick by Brick · 2013;in:right"
One study, in the book Brick by Brick, later did the maths for nineteen ninety-three to two thousand and **two**.

// CARD a4-08 · SEE: LEGO's dark history gallery behind; profit bars rise year by year 1993–2002 above a zero line (1998 dips red); on "destroyed" a blue VALUE line sinks under them to −$1.6B; on "down" it holds · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@profit_vs_value;bg:lego_history_gallery;years:1993,2002;loss:1998;go:reported;vgo:destroyed;val:−$1.6B;vlab:ECONOMIC VALUE;lab:ACCOUNTING PROFIT, EACH YEAR;out:right"
LEGO reported profits in almost every one of those years, yet destroyed about one point six billion US dollars of **value**.
Profits on paper. Value going **down**.

// CARD a4-09 · SEE: a warehouse of pallets dim behind; one new brick drops in; on each named cost a heavy tag clamps onto it with a chain and it sinks lower: MOULD, COLOUR, SUPPLIER, SHELF SPACE · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@tax_chain;bg:lego_clip_warehouse_packed_aisle;chips:MOULD@mould~COLOUR@colour~SUPPLIER@supplier~SHELF SPACE@shelf;go:piece;lab:THE COMPLEXITY TAX;in:right"
That's the complexity tax: every new piece charges the whole system, the mould, the colour, the supplier, the shelf **space**.

// CARD a4-10 · MOTIF · SEE: a dim warehouse stacked to the roof (AI moment art); the brick drops onto the floor in front of the stacks; GROWTH THAT MAKES YOU POORER sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_art_warehouse;go:growth;x:.5;y:.66;h:.11;lab:GROWTH THAT MAKES YOU POORER;lx:.5;z:.3;out:right"
When the tax is bigger than what a piece earns, growth makes you **poorer**.
